# A Small Lump - a vet, an old dog, and an owner who cannot afford the answer.
# Written for the library. One person, one animal that cannot speak, and a question about whose decision it is.

title: A Small Lump
start: start

== start ==
bg east_corner
music room_tone
The appointment is at ten past four and it is a lump, which is most of what a small animal
practice does in a week: somebody has found something and they want to be told it is nothing.
You have been doing it for eleven years and you can tell a fatty lump through the skin the way
you can tell a full stop from a comma, and you tell people that it is nothing about forty times
a week and you are right about thirty-nine of them.
-> the_dog

== the_dog ==
The dog is a lurcher of about thirteen and he is called Bramble and he lies on the bench with his
chin on his paws and lets you do whatever you like, which at thirteen is usually because he has
decided you are not interesting.
The owner is eighty-one and came on the bus and he has the dog's basket under the chair rather
than leave it in the waiting room, and he has been to us twice before - once for the ears and once,
four years ago, when the dog ate a whole Christmas cake and had to be made to be sick.
-> the_lump

== the_lump ==
The lump is on the inside of the back leg and it is not a fatty lump. It is fixed to the muscle
underneath and it does not move when you push it and it is about the size of a walnut, and the
dog does not flinch, which tells you less than people think.
The honest answer is that you need a needle in it and a laboratory, and that costs a hundred and
forty pounds before anybody has decided anything, and you know what the man's face is going to do
before you have said the number.
-> the_knowing

== the_knowing ==
If it comes back as the thing you think it is, the operation is six hundred and he is thirteen
and he is a lurcher, which is a dog with no fat on it anywhere, and the recovery is hard on a dog
with no fat on it.
If it comes back as something else, you have spent a hundred and forty pounds of an old man's
money to be told to leave it alone.
And if you do nothing at all, it will do what it does, and it will probably take about four
months, and for most of those four months the dog will not know anything is wrong.
-> the_decision

== the_decision ==
You are standing at the bench with your hands still on the dog and the man is sitting on the
chair with his hat in his hands and he has not asked anything yet, which means he is waiting.
* Give him the number first, before anything else. -> the_number
* Tell him what you can see, and then the number. -> the_seeing
* Offer to do the needle on the practice, at no charge, and leave the decision for later. -> the_free
* Tell him to take the dog home and think about it, and put nothing in writing. -> the_home

== the_number ==
you: The test is a hundred and forty pounds. If it comes back as the thing I think it is, the
operation is six hundred, and I would not be doing the operation.
He nods, which is what they do, and then he asks the question that is the whole of it and which
nobody ever asks in the right order.
man: Which one of those is for him?
-> the_which

== the_seeing ==
You take your hands off the dog and you sit down on the other chair, which you almost never do,
and you tell him all of it in the plainest words you have: what it is, what the options are, what
the numbers are, and what each of them is for.
He listens the whole way through without once looking at the dog, which is how you know he is
frightened, and at the end of it he says the thing about the basket.
-> the_basket

== the_basket ==
man: He's got a basket in the room where I watch the television. He's had it eleven years.
He says it as though it were an argument about the lump, and it is, and it is a better one than
most of what gets said in this room.
-> the_which

== the_free ==
you: I'll do the needle now, and I won't charge you for it, and we'll know in three days.
He looks at you for a long moment and then he says no, thank you, and he says it politely, and
when you ask him why he tells you the truth, which is that he has spent his whole life paying for
things and he is not going to start being given them at eighty-one.
-> END

== the_home ==
You write nothing down. You tell him it is probably nothing and to keep an eye on it, and you
book him in for a fortnight, and he thanks you and puts the lead on and pays the consultation
fee at the desk and goes to get the bus with the basket under his arm.
-> ending_the_notes

== the_which ==
you: None of them is for him. He doesn't know what a needle is and he doesn't know what six
hundred pounds is. All three of those are for you.
man: Then I'll ask it again. Which one is for me.
-> the_answer

== the_answer ==
You tell him the truth, which is that the answer depends on whether he can live with four months
of not knowing, or two weeks of knowing and then four months of a different kind of not knowing,
and that both of those are worse than the dog's four months, and that you cannot make that choice
for him because you do not have to live in his house afterwards.
He takes his hat off the chair and thanks you, and it is a real thank you, and it is the only one
you will get out of the next four months.
-> ending_the_answer

== ending_the_answer ==
He decides. Whatever he decides is his own, and it was never yours to make, and the whole of the
job - the sitting down, the plain words, the four minutes of silence afterwards - was to keep it
that way rather than letting it become a thing that happened to him in a room he did not choose.
He comes back in the March with the basket under his arm and no dog, and he has come to tell you
rather than to ask you anything, and he sits down in the waiting room without an appointment and
waits the whole of the surgery to do it. You see him at twenty past six. It takes four minutes.
-> END

== ending_the_notes ==
What you wrote in the notes was that the lump was probably benign and to monitor it, which is
what you said out loud, and it is not untrue.
He books the fortnight at the desk on his way out and he puts the basket under his arm and he
says thank you very much, and you are the eleventh year of doing this and you know exactly what
you have just done, which is that you have handed an eighty-one-year-old man a fortnight of
peace in exchange for a fortnight of it being somebody else's problem, and that the somebody
else is you, and that you will be in this room when he comes back.
He comes back in the fortnight and the lump is bigger and he says cheerfully that he knew that,
and the question he asks you now is the one he could have asked at ten past four, and it is
late, and it is still better than not answering it, and you answer it.
-> END
